<h2 id="specifying-sizes" oldids='size-keywords'>
Specifying Box Sizes</h2>

ISSUE: [[css-sizing-3#specifying-sizes]]

<h3 id="sizing-properties">
Sizing Properties</h3>

ISSUE: [[css-sizing-3#sizing-properties]]

	<pre class="propdef shorthand">
	Name: size
	Value: <<'width'>> <<'height'>>?
	Initial: auto
	Applies to: all elements
	</pre>

	The 'size' property is a [=shorthand=]
	that sets 'width' and 'height' in a single declaration.
	If the second value is omitted,
	it is copied from the first.

	The 'size' shorthand cannot be used in ''@page'' contexts,
	as ''@page'' already has an unrelated '@page/size' descriptor
	for setting the page size.

	Issue: The 'size' property needs to be omitted from the [=preferred shorthand order=] in CSSOM,
	to avoid compat problems and conflicts with ''@page''.

	<pre class="propdef shorthand">
	Name: min-size
	Value: <<'min-width'>> <<'min-height'>>?
	Initial: auto
	Applies to: all elements
	</pre>

	The 'min-size' property is a [=shorthand=]
	that sets 'min-width' and 'min-height' in a single declaration.
	If the second value is omitted,
	it is copied from the first.

	<pre class="propdef shorthand">
	Name: max-size
	Value: <<'max-width'>> <<'max-height'>>?
	Initial: none
	Applies to: all elements
	</pre>

	The 'max-size' property is a [=shorthand=]
	that sets 'max-width' and 'max-height' in a single declaration.
	If the second value is omitted,
	it is copied from the first.

<h3 id="sizing-values" oldids='width-height-keywords'>
New Sizing Values: the ''contain'', ''stretch()'', ''fit-content()'', and ''width/calc-size()'' values</h3>

ISSUE: Integrate [[css-sizing-3#sizing-values]]

	Level 4 adds the ability to use
	the ''width/contain'', ''width/fit-content()'', and ''width/calc-size()'' values
	in the [=sizing properties=],
	thus expanding the <<box-size>> production as follows:

	<pre class=prod>
		<dfn><<box-size>></dfn> = <<length-percentage [0,∞]>>
		             | stretch | contain | min-content | max-content | fit-content
		             | stretch(<<length-percentage [0,∞]>>) | fit-content(<<length-percentage [0,∞]>>)
		             | <<calc-size()>>
	</pre>

	<dl dfn-type=value dfn-for="width, height, inline-size, block-size, min-width, min-height, min-inline-size, min-block-size, max-width, max-height, max-inline-size, max-block-size">
		<dt><dfn function lt="stretch()">stretch(<<length-percentage [0,∞]>>)</dfn>
		<dd>
			Fit the [=margin box=] to the size specified by the argument.
			For this purpose, treat ''margin/auto'' [=margins=] as zero,
			and ignore <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/CSS2/box.html#collapsing-margins">margin collapsing</a>, 'margin-trim',
			and other effects that suppress or truncate the margin.

			Note: Although margin collapsing, etc. are ignored
			for the purpose of calculating this size,
			their effects are not otherwise suppressed,
			and apply as usual.

			The <<length-percentage>> argument is resolved
			exactly as for <<length-percentage>> values standing alone.

			ISSUE(5715): Open discussion wrt details here.

		<dt><dfn function lt="fit-content()">fit-content(<<length-percentage [0,∞]>>)</dfn>
		<dd>
			Use the fit-content formula
			with the <a>available space</a> replaced by ''stretch()'' into the specified argument,
			i.e.
			<code>min(''width/max-content'', max(''width/min-content'', stretch(<<length-percentage>>))</code>.

			<wpt>
				auto-scrollbar-inside-stf-abspos.html
				block-fit-content-as-initial.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-001.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-002.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-003.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-004.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-005.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-006.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-007.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-008.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-009.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-010.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-011.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-012.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-013.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-014.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-015.html
				fit-content-length-percentage-016.html
			</wpt>

		<dt><dfn>contain</dfn>
		<dd>
			If the box has a [=preferred aspect ratio=],
			applies [=contain-fit sizing=],
			attempting to fit into the box’s constraints
			while maintaining its [=preferred aspect ratio=] insofar as possible.
			See [[#contain-fit-sizing]].

			If the box has no [=preferred aspect ratio=],
			applies [=stretch-fit sizing=].

		<dt>''calc-size()''
		<dd>
			See [[#calc-size]].

			Note: The ''max-width/none'' keyword is
			not usable within ''max-width/calc-size()''.
	</dl>

	Note: These new values add to the set of values
	that the definition of <</calc-size()>>
	refers to as “allowed in the context”.

